{#
	The byline at the foot of an article. Figma `author-box` / `author-card` on
	`blog / single` (21:189), new in the 2026-09 revision.

	Derived, not authored — like `next-project`, `breadcrumbs` and
	`related-posts` it comes from what the post already knows. WordPress has
	carried a name, a biography and an avatar for every user since it had
	users, and they are edited on the profile screen the author already owns.
	An ACF group repeating them would be a second copy to keep in sync, and the
	one that goes stale is always the copy.

	DOM order is avatar → text. In the frame the avatar sits at x=672, the
	right-hand end of the card, which under dir="rtl" is the *start* edge and
	therefore the first child. Writing the text first would mirror the card.

	The avatar is decorative: the name is the next thing read, so describing
	the picture as well would say it twice. With avatars switched off in
	Settings the circle renders empty, exactly as the frame draws it.

	Params:
	  author  Timber\User — the post's author.
#}

{% if author %}
	{% set bio = author.meta('description') %}

	<aside class="author-box">
		<div class="container author-box__inner">
			<div class="author-card">
				<span class="author-card__avatar">
					{% if author.avatar({ size: 128 }) %}
						<img
							src="{{ author.avatar({ size: 128 }) }}"
							alt=""
							width="64"
							height="64"
							loading="lazy"
							decoding="async"
						>
					{% endif %}
				</span>

				<div class="author-card__text">
					<p class="author-card__name"><bdi>{{ author.name }}</bdi></p>

					{% if bio %}
						<p class="author-card__bio">{{ bio }}</p>
					{% endif %}
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</aside>
{% endif %}
